The '''National Universal Health Act''' (Okaiken: 国民皆医療法, ''Kokumin Kai-iryō Hō''), often referred to simply as the '''Universal Health Act''', is a landmark piece of legislation in [[Okaiken]]. The Act transitions the country from a multi-payer statutory insurance system to a comprehensive, single-payer universal healthcare system, providing free medical care to all Sanese citizens.

The Act was the central campaign promise of [[Prime Minister of Okaiken|Prime Minister]] [[Shigetsuma Honda]] from the [[Okaiken general election, 2021|2021 general election]] and became the cornerstone and most controversial achievement of his first term in government.

== Legislative history ==

=== Background ===
Prior to the Act, Okaiken operated on a multi-payer universal statutory health insurance system. While this system provided universal coverage, it relied on a complex network of employment-based and residential insurance plans, requiring co-payments (typically 30%) and premiums from citizens.

The [[Progressive Party of Okaiken]], under the leadership of [[Shigetsuma Honda]], made the transition to a "true" single-payer system—funded entirely by general taxation and providing care "free at the point of use"—a central plank of its 2021 platform.

=== Passage ===
After forming a minority government in 2021, the Honda administration spent nearly two years drafting the complex legislation. The bill was formally introduced to the [[House of Commoners of Okaiken|House of Commoners]] on May 10, 2023.

The introduction followed a 2023 upper house election which saw the Progressive Party gain seats, a result which the Honda administration interpreted as a public mandate to proceed.

The bill faced months of intense and bitter debate in the Diet. It was filibustered for weeks by the opposition [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|LDP]], who forced clause-by-clause votes on nearly every provision. Despite the procedural challenges, the PPO, with support from the [[Social Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Social Democratic Party]] and a few independents, successfully passed the bill on October 20, 2023. It passed the [[House of Officers]] in December and was given Royal Assent by Emperor Masahito, becoming law on December 22, 2023.

== Key provisions ==
The National Universal Health Act is a sweeping reform of the Sanese healthcare system. Its primary provisions include:

* '''Establishment of a Single-Payer System:''' The Act dissolves the existing multi-payer insurance co-operatives and employment-based plans, replacing them with a single government-run entity: the '''Okaiken National Health Service (ONHS)'''.
* '''Universal and Automatic Coverage:''' All Sanese citizens and permanent residents are automatically enrolled in the ONHS.
* '''Comprehensive Benefits:''' The ONHS is mandated to cover all "medically necessary" care, including doctor's visits, hospital stays, surgical procedures, preventative care, and maternity care.
* '''Free at the Point of Use:''' The Act abolishes all co-payments, deductibles, and premiums for all covered services.
* '''Funding Mechanism:''' The system is funded entirely through general taxation, including a new, highly controversial "Health and Welfare Levy" on high-income earners and corporations.
* '''Phasing of Additional Benefits:''' Coverage for dental, vision, and prescription drugs is set to be phased in over a five-year period.
* '''Role of Private Insurance:''' The Act prohibits the sale of private insurance for services covered by the ONHS, limiting the private market to supplemental coverage only.

== Support and opposition ==
The Act is the primary political dividing line in 21st-century Okaiken.

=== Support ===
Proponents, led by Prime Minister [[Shigetsuma Honda]], argue that healthcare is a fundamental human right and that the Act is a "moral necessity." They criticized the "two-tier" nature of the previous system, where access to care was dictated by employment status. Supporters also point to long-term savings from a massive reduction in administrative overhead.

=== Opposition ===
Opposition was vocal and organized, led by the conservative [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|LDP]] and the national business federation. Critics warned that the transition would lead to "soaring government spending and unsustainable debt," pointing to the global inflation of 2023-2024 as evidence that the government could not afford the program.

Fiscal conservatives and business federations argued that the new taxes required to fund the ONHS would make Sanese businesses uncompetitive. Furthermore, the Okaiken Medical Association expressed concern over government-set fee schedules and the risk of long wait times. This criticism formed the core of the LDP's "fiscal sanity" platform during the [[Okaiken general election, 2025|2025 general election]].

== Implementation ==
The Act outlined a phased two-year rollout, which began on April 1, 2024. The full transition to the Okaiken National Health Service (ONHS) is scheduled to be complete by April 1, 2026.

Following the [[Okaiken general election, 2025|2025 election]], the new coalition government's [[Okaiken Socialist Party|OSP]] leader, [[Rento Nakayama]], was appointed as Minister of Health and Welfare to oversee the final stages of its implementation.

